From 6b1895a276832ff8fa263e090e424b0aa6d12e3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 16:35:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 102/105] packetizer: dts: let a break cost one frame, not the
 rest of the block

Same as the a/52 packetizer: what is left of a block once a frame has been
taken out of it goes back to the caller and comes straight back here, still
flagged, so the parser is flushed again and the second flush arrives with the
block's timestamp already spent - and an undated block with no running date is
released outright by the "wait for the first PTS" gate just below.

Measured on a four second dca item in a program stream looped three times with
--input-repeat on the dummy sink: frames handed to the output per pass go from
374 to 375, samples from 191488 to 192000, output frames stamped
DISCONTINUITY from two per seam to one, and flushes per seam from three to
two. One frame per loop here rather than the a/52 figure only because the
program stream puts a single frame in each pes; a block holding several costs
several.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 modules/packetizer/dts.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/modules/packetizer/dts.c b/modules/packetizer/dts.c
index e3a4a92cb1..c08a3fff69 100644
--- a/modules/packetizer/dts.c
+++ b/modules/packetizer/dts.c
@@ -150,6 +150,16 @@ static block_t *PacketizeBlock( decoder_t *p_dec, block_t **pp_block )
                 block_Release( p_block );
                 return NULL;
             }
+
+            /* What is left of this block once a frame has been taken out of
+             * it comes straight back here, and the break belongs to the seam
+             * the block arrived on, not to the rest of the block: leaving the
+             * flag set makes every following frame flush the parser again,
+             * and the second flush arrives with the block's timestamp already
+             * spent, so the remainder is dropped for want of one - and each
+             * flush arms the outbound flag again, stamping another frame the
+             * decoder then acts on. */
+            p_block->i_flags &= ~BLOCK_FLAG_DISCONTINUITY;
         }
 
         if ( !date_Get( &p_sys->end_date ) && p_block->i_pts <= VLC_TICK_INVALID ) {
